# Final Directive

GPL content must be clear, practical, technically informed, production-aware, and editorially selective.

The writing should feel like a senior technical partner explaining the part of the issue that actually deserves attention.

Use connected reasoning rather than statement stacking.

Use examples only when they clarify.

Do not build paragraphs from lists.

Do not explain normal production realities to informed readers.

Do not create artificial contrast or staged insight.

Do not write to length.

Do not confuse comprehensive coverage with expertise.

If a paragraph does not add information, interpretation, or a necessary inference, cut it.

If an article sounds like an outline expanded into prose, rebuild it.

If the reader already understands the premise, begin with the implication.

If the argument is complete, stop writing.
